WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:05.389
Now, let's break down a practical example of how we can take these concepts and build

2
00:00:05.389 --> 00:00:09.789
a typical piece of UI functionality in the Unreal editor.

3
00:00:09.789 --> 00:00:14.230
We can see that we've got dynamically spawned widgets over a world actor, with both procedural

4
00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:16.329
and baked animations.

5
00:00:16.329 --> 00:00:20.760
Their float direction is randomized but their float distance is not.

6
00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:24.519
Let's break down how we've used a blend of code and editor work to achieve this

7
00:00:24.519 --> 00:00:26.570
effect.

8
00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:31.769
The first thing we need to do is navigate to the UI floating damage asset in the editor.

9
00:00:31.769 --> 00:00:34.890
This is under the UI UMG folder.

10
00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:37.179
Let's break down what we can see here.

11
00:00:37.179 --> 00:00:41.409
Firstly, the widget is parented to the floating damage widget.

12
00:00:41.409 --> 00:00:45.409
This is our native class and it's here that will trigger our functionality.

13
00:00:45.409 --> 00:00:48.890
Breaking down the hierarchy view, we can see we've got a single text block inside

14
00:00:48.890 --> 00:00:50.960
of a border container.

15
00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:55.740
We're using 88 as our placeholder text as 88 is the widest two digit number that we

16
00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:57.409
can get.

17
00:00:57.409 --> 00:01:01.590
At the bottom, we can see we've already got a baked, authored animation called spawn

18
00:01:01.590 --> 00:01:03.929
animation.

19
00:01:03.929 --> 00:01:08.109
Clicking through the tracks, we can see we're only animating the alpha of the content

20
00:01:08.109 --> 00:01:10.269
parameter of the border.

21
00:01:10.269 --> 00:01:15.719
This is basically going to take our initially opaque text and blend it out entirely.

22
00:01:15.719 --> 00:01:18.920
Although this looks like a linear blend, if we click through to the curves menu,

23
00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:28.420
we can actually define a different curve between these two keyframes.

24
00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:32.340
Damage text is actually a bind widget, one of the year property specifiers we covered

25
00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:34.099
earlier in this talk.

26
00:01:34.099 --> 00:01:38.700
If we rename it from damage text to a random string, we can see that the blueprint no longer

27
00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:39.700
compiles.

28
00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:43.659
Of course, renaming this widget to the expected value fixes up the binding and we're good

29
00:01:43.659 --> 00:01:48.299
to go.

30
00:01:48.299 --> 00:01:51.459
Moving quickly off to the event graph, we can see that we've got absolutely no logic

31
00:01:51.459 --> 00:01:54.760
at play here, all of our functionality is driven through the code.

32
00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:58.819
So let's jump into Visual Studio and break down the underlying float damage widget

33
00:01:58.819 --> 00:02:00.340
class.

34
00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:03.939
So straight away, there's a few things we can point out.

35
00:02:03.939 --> 00:02:08.840
We've got the usual Uclass, generated.h and generated body macros, meaning we're dealing

36
00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:10.680
with a reflected type.

37
00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:14.240
We cover this heavily in the Introduction to C++ in Unreal talk.

38
00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:18.000
As a brief overview though, we can be sure that the instances of this class are not

39
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:21.400
going to get garbage collected when they shouldn't be, Unreal's keeping a reference

40
00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:23.349
to them.

41
00:02:23.349 --> 00:02:26.590
We can also see that we're extending off of user widget, as we mentioned at the start

42
00:02:26.590 --> 00:02:28.439
of this talk.

43
00:02:28.439 --> 00:02:32.039
Moving on from there, we've got a couple of publicly accessible functions in our header,

44
00:02:32.039 --> 00:02:35.139
and we'll be using those to drive some of our gameplay.

45
00:02:35.139 --> 00:02:39.800
Next, we're overriding some critical functions as outlined earlier, that being the native

46
00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:43.599
construct and the native tick.

47
00:02:43.599 --> 00:02:47.539
Following that we've got our bindings, that being the damage text and the spawn animation

48
00:02:47.539 --> 00:02:52.520
bindings as bind widget and bind widget animation respectively.

49
00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:56.120
Then finally, right at the bottom, we've got a few runtime members to help us drive

50
00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:57.719
our gameplay.

51
00:02:57.719 --> 00:03:01.979
Take note of the U function markup on the on spawn anim over function.

52
00:03:01.979 --> 00:03:04.960
This is going to be important in just a moment.

53
00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:08.919
Moving over to our CPP, we've got a fairly standard constructor.

54
00:03:08.919 --> 00:03:12.460
Our bindable properties are initialized as null because our design layer is going

55
00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:16.659
to populate those for us, and our float direction is initialized here given a random

56
00:03:16.659 --> 00:03:18.360
X and Y value.

57
00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:23.139
This is how we introduce that random float direction on a per widget basis.

58
00:03:23.139 --> 00:03:26.860
In the body of the constructor, we're creating a delegate binding by passing in both

59
00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:31.659
the name of a function, in this case on spawn anim over, and the object on which

60
00:03:31.659 --> 00:03:34.009
to call that function.

61
00:03:34.009 --> 00:03:37.050
This is why the U function markup was so important.

62
00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:42.090
One of the most useful things about reflection is its ability to find a function by name.

63
00:03:42.090 --> 00:03:46.009
Once this delegate is invoked, it's going to parse over whatever object we pass in

64
00:03:46.009 --> 00:03:49.009
for a function called on spawn anim over.

65
00:03:49.009 --> 00:03:51.979
If it finds one, it's going to call it.

66
00:03:51.979 --> 00:03:56.379
Moving on from there, we've got our native construct, our begin play of the widget

67
00:03:56.379 --> 00:03:57.960
world.

68
00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:01.439
We check to see if the animation has been successfully burned from the design layer,

69
00:04:01.439 --> 00:04:03.419
as it's a pointer after all.

70
00:04:03.419 --> 00:04:06.699
If it is, we proceed through the following steps to make sure our widget both looks

71
00:04:06.699 --> 00:04:09.289
and behaves as expected.

72
00:04:09.289 --> 00:04:13.729
Firstly, we set its alignment in the viewport, and this is essentially going to move the

73
00:04:13.729 --> 00:04:17.490
widget's route from the standard top left to the center of the text, making for

74
00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:19.209
a more aesthetic effect.

75
00:04:19.209 --> 00:04:22.379
Next, we actually bind that delegate.

76
00:04:22.379 --> 00:04:26.660
We call the native bindToAnimationFinished function, and pass in the animation we wish

77
00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:29.379
to bind to and a copy of the delegate.

78
00:04:29.379 --> 00:04:34.339
As you'd expect, when the animation is finished, we're going to get our onSpawnAnimationOver

79
00:04:34.339 --> 00:04:35.339
function invoked.

80
00:04:35.339 --> 00:04:38.889
Finally, we simply play the animation.

81
00:04:38.889 --> 00:04:42.490
So now the widget's been constructed, let's deal with that floating effect.

82
00:04:42.490 --> 00:04:46.430
Firstly, we grab a reference to the local player controller.

83
00:04:46.430 --> 00:04:51.269
If we were on a server, the getFirstPlayerController function would simply return the first connected

84
00:04:51.269 --> 00:04:52.269
client.

85
00:04:52.269 --> 00:04:55.949
However, because we're definitely on the client, calling getFirstPlayerController is

86
00:04:55.949 --> 00:04:58.910
guaranteed to return our local client.

87
00:04:58.910 --> 00:05:02.550
Now that we've got that local controller, we can go ahead and project a world location

88
00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:05.879
onto their screen, giving us a screen coordinate.

89
00:05:05.879 --> 00:05:11.139
In this case, we're just passing in the location in screen space as a ref.

90
00:05:11.139 --> 00:05:14.519
Setting the position in viewport does exactly as you'd expect.

91
00:05:14.519 --> 00:05:17.879
It's going to place our widget over the actor that we're dealing damage to, however

92
00:05:17.879 --> 00:05:23.680
we want to offset that value by some value over time, giving us that float effect.

93
00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:28.120
We simply take that projected coordinate and offset it by an accumulated distance multiplied

94
00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:30.439
by a random float direction.

95
00:05:30.439 --> 00:05:34.680
We'll keep accumulating that value until we eventually get a callback from our animation

96
00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:41.180
that it's finished.

97
00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:45.579
We can see that in our onSpawnAnimOver function, we're simply reporting something in the log

98
00:05:45.579 --> 00:05:49.500
so we can keep on top of things, and then removing it from the viewport.

99
00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:53.000
As the viewport is this widget linked to the root set and the widget is a reflected

100
00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:56.680
member, we can rest assured that it will eventually get hoovered up by the garbage collector

101
00:05:56.680 --> 00:05:57.680
from this point onward.

102
00:05:57.680 --> 00:06:02.560
Finally, we can take a look at those public functions and honestly, there's nothing fancy.

103
00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:07.959
The setDamageValue function simply takes in a float and coerces it into an fText.

104
00:06:07.959 --> 00:06:11.800
Text blocks take an fText type as to afford us a chance to localize whatever text we

105
00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:12.800
want to display.

106
00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:16.319
Of course, in this instance, we're just displaying a float, so we can simply get

107
00:06:16.319 --> 00:06:21.860
away with calling the fTextStaticAsNumber function without having to worry about any localization.

108
00:06:21.860 --> 00:06:25.879
The setDamageLocation function is simpler still, it's just a setter.

109
00:06:25.879 --> 00:06:29.139
The cache damage location is what we'll use in the native tick to make sure the

110
00:06:29.139 --> 00:06:32.519
widget remains over the hit actor in world space.

111
00:06:32.519 --> 00:06:34.560
And that's it, nothing crazy.

112
00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:38.000
We've triggered a bound animation, we've built a procedural animation driven by a

113
00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:41.560
tick, and we've bound to a widget for dynamically setting text values.

114
00:06:41.560 --> 00:06:44.959
Let's quickly take a look at where we trigger this widget's creation, and where

115
00:06:44.959 --> 00:06:48.529
do we take advantage of those public functions.

116
00:06:48.529 --> 00:06:52.810
Jumping into the explosion component, which was a component added as part of the Introduction

117
00:06:52.810 --> 00:06:57.029
to Gameplay talk in this course, we can see our new block of code.

118
00:06:57.029 --> 00:07:01.250
Firstly, we have our NotUeServerPreProcessor.

119
00:07:01.250 --> 00:07:04.889
This means this entire block of code will be stripped out of server builds, ensuring

120
00:07:04.889 --> 00:07:08.579
we don't end up with UI on the server.

121
00:07:08.579 --> 00:07:11.740
This is followed by an authority check to make sure that we're definitely not on

122
00:07:11.740 --> 00:07:16.019
the server, and you'll remember from the Introduction to C++ talk that editor targets

123
00:07:16.019 --> 00:07:19.339
contain both client and server code.

124
00:07:19.339 --> 00:07:23.500
It's important that we have both a preprocessor and an authority check to make sure that

125
00:07:23.500 --> 00:07:25.959
all potential targets are accounted for.

126
00:07:25.959 --> 00:07:30.680
Next, we cache the location of the actor, and check its bounds to calculate an aesthetically

127
00:07:30.680 --> 00:07:33.699
pleasing spawn location in world space.

128
00:07:33.699 --> 00:07:37.620
This value, along with the amount of damage this actor took, is passed through to the

129
00:07:37.620 --> 00:07:40.959
new SpawnFloatingIndicator function.

130
00:07:40.959 --> 00:07:43.060
It's here that we do our actual spawning.

131
00:07:43.060 --> 00:07:46.959
We check to see if we've got a valid floating damage class, and that the amount of damage

132
00:07:46.959 --> 00:07:51.259
passed in was not zero, as displaying zero damage is basically pointless.

133
00:07:51.259 --> 00:07:54.800
From there, we call the templatedCreateWidget function that we mentioned in today's

134
00:07:54.800 --> 00:08:00.069
talk, as well as a sensible outer, in this case it's just going to be the world.

135
00:08:00.069 --> 00:08:04.870
Once we've made sure we've got a valid pointer, we call both the setDamageValue and the setDamageLocation

136
00:08:04.870 --> 00:08:08.269
functions to make sure our widget has got the correct appearance.

137
00:08:08.269 --> 00:08:13.089
Finally, we call addToViewport with a z-value of 1.

138
00:08:13.089 --> 00:08:17.529
Anything with a z-value of 0 or below is going to appear behind this widget, and

139
00:08:17.529 --> 00:08:22.259
of course anything with a z-value higher is going to appear in front of this widget.

140
00:08:22.259 --> 00:08:26.180
We've now got an animated damage indicator, accurately displaying how much damage an

141
00:08:26.180 --> 00:08:28.610
exploding actor takes.

142
00:08:28.610 --> 00:08:32.559
That concludes Introduction to Unreal Motion Graphics for Programmers.

143
00:08:32.559 --> 00:08:36.059
Today we learned about the UWidget and the UserWidget.

144
00:08:36.059 --> 00:08:39.779
We covered authoring new widgets using the Widget Blueprint Editor and its inbuilt

145
00:08:39.779 --> 00:08:41.919
Animation Editor.

146
00:08:41.919 --> 00:08:45.799
We looked at widget-overrideable functions and the special UProperties we have at

147
00:08:45.799 --> 00:08:46.799
our disposal.

148
00:08:46.799 --> 00:08:50.919
And we also learned how we can use the 3D widgets to build UI that can be used in

149
00:08:50.919 --> 00:08:53.840
unconventional projects such as VR.

150
00:08:53.840 --> 00:08:58.720
We wrapped up today's session with a live example of a piece of UI built in UMG, walking

151
00:08:58.840 --> 00:09:03.490
through how we can build UI code that's responsive to designer requirements.

152
00:09:03.490 --> 00:09:05.490
Thank you so much for watching, and have a wonderful day.
